{
  "requirement": "The harness MUST expose an `outline` tool with this interface:\n\n```text\noutline({ file: String }) -> Array<OutlineEntry>\n\nOutlineEntry {\n    item: String,\n    kind: String,\n    name: String?,\n    line_start: Integer,\n    line_end: Integer,\n    children: Array<OutlineEntry>,\n}\n```\n\nThe tool MUST return the outline of a permitted file in a supported language\nunder {cite}`requirements.references.outlines`, with each node's selector under\n{cite}`requirements.references.outline_selectors`, its kind, its name when it is\nnamed, and its current inclusive line range, nested by outline ancestry. Results\nMUST contain no source text and MUST NOT become evidence inputs; the model reads\nthe nodes it relies on with the `habeas` tool. For a file using the plain-text\nprofile, the outline lists its `line` nodes. Explicitly requesting an excluded\nfile MUST be handled as a denial under {cite}`requirements.authoring.read_denial`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the sources satisfy `requirements.authoring.outline_tool`.**\n\n**Implementation**\n\n- **Interface.** `definitions()` in `src/authoring/tools.rs` advertises an `outline` tool. Its schema has a required string `file` and `additionalProperties: false`. Its description states the entry fields, the supported languages, the plain-text `line` profile, that no source text is returned, and the denial and outside-project behavior. `dispatch` routes `\"outline\"` to `outline()`.\n- **Entry shape.** `outline_entries()` emits `item` (the node's full selector), `kind`, `name` (`display_name()`, which is null for anonymous nodes), `line_start`, `line_end`, and recursively nested `children`. These match `OutlineEntry`. The result is the array of the root node's children, so nesting follows outline ancestry.\n- **Selectors and line ranges.** The selectors, hashes, `#N` disambiguation and line ranges are built in `src/outline/mod.rs`. That code includes attached doc comments and attributes in `line_start`/`line_end`. It is implemented and tested under `references.outlines` and `references.outline_selectors`, which I did not reassess.\n- **Language and plain-text profiles.** `OutlineCache::parse` uses `outline::parse`, which selects the profile by case-insensitive extension. All other files go to the plain-text profile, where each non-blank line becomes an anonymous `line@hash` node.\n- **No source text, no evidence inputs.** The `ToolDelivery` has `inputs: Vec::new()`, and the entries contain only metadata.\n- **Denial.**\n  - `parse_file` calls `access.file(...)?` before parsing. Denial of an excluded project file therefore propagates as `PermissionDenied`, which is the abort path under `read_denial`.\n  - `dispatch` passes errors through `recoverable_os_denial` and `recoverable_outside_request`. Only unpermitted outside-project paths become ordinary tool errors, as `read_denial` specifies.\n- **Parse failures.** A syntax error or an unreadable file becomes an ordinary tool error carrying the line-reporting message from the outline layer. The requirement does not say what happens in this case, and this behavior comes from `references.outlines` (\"a source with any syntax error MUST fail to parse\").\n- **Marking.** The `{impl}` marks cover every code path involved: `outline`, `outline_entries`, `definitions`, `dispatch`, `parse_file`, `recoverable_outside_request` and `OutlineNode::display_name`. I found no unmarked implementing code and no extra behavior beyond what this requirement and the definitions it cites specify.\n\n**Tests**\n\n- `outline_tool_is_advertised_with_a_required_file_argument` checks the exact parameter schema and that the description mentions the key fields and \"no source text\".\n- `outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs` covers most of the behavior:\n  - an exact JSON comparison of a Rust outline, including nesting (`impl` → `fn`) and an attached doc comment extending `line_start` to 1;\n  - a check that the doc text does not appear in the result;\n  - empty `inputs`;\n  - a plain-text file, where blank lines are skipped, every entry is a `line` with a null name and a `line@` selector, and the line numbers are correct;\n  - failures for a missing file and for missing arguments;\n  - after a `disallow` rule is added, an explicitly requested excluded file gives `PermissionDenied`, which is the abort path.\n- `unpermitted_requests_outside_the_project_are_recoverable_tool_errors` covers the `outline` tool with an outside-project path (recoverable, no inputs recorded) and with a matching `disallow` (still aborts). It exercises the outside-project clause of `read_denial` for the outline tool.\n- Per-language outline content, syntax-error reporting and selector rules are tested in `src/outline/tests.rs` under the cited requirements.\n\nThe tests cover the interface, nesting, line ranges, the plain-text profile, text exclusion, evidence exclusion, and the denial and failure cases. I found no acknowledged gaps (no FIXME markers) and no unacknowledged ones.",
